# What the option file reader still parses or writes by itself, and the one
# guard that is kept.  Checked by tests/check-number-fields.sh.
#
#   rule|file|function|count|pass|reason
#
# "count" is the number of matches in the function (for rule 1, in the
# file); it may only fall.  "pass" is the pass that moves it onto the owner
# (P2, P4) and "keep" for one that is not a number the owner keeps, with its
# reason.  A file with a rule 1 line is read in source form instead of by nm
# until its last parser is gone.  --strict ignores every line but the keep
# ones.

# --- the option file reader, moved onto the owner with the file pass (P2) ---
R1|we_opt.c|(file)|22|P2|five of these are the editor numbers (P2); the other seventeen are the option file's other integer keys, moved in P4 (CompilerSwitch is read twice: the language entry and the user's own copy)
R2|we_opt.c|WpeReadGeneral|5|P2|the five editor numbers are read with atoi and written into the editor

# --- a buffer built without an editor ---
R4|we_buffer.c|buffer_tab_width|2|keep|the buffer owner is pure and unit tested without an editor: it falls back to its own default for a tab width of 0 or less
